Z||000005||lennyb4||11-17-1999||08:21 AM||||O.K. I looked at my cylinder last night. The only problem is that it looks like a cheap aftermarket plastic part. Also i could not find the bleeder on it anywhere.I do know that it is not leeking anywhere. Any idea's?||||reg||9
Z||000006||DJRice||11-17-1999||11:16 AM||||GM has been using plastic master cylinders in all kinds of stuff for years, but if it looks like it is newer than the rest of the car, then it is possible that the person who installed it did not bleed the clutch properly.  Fiero Clutches are very picky about how they are bled.  <P>Refer to the previously posted link for all you ever wanted to know about the Fiero's clutch.<P>I forgot to add that the bleeder is on the slave cylinder under the trunk lid...<p>[This message has been edited by DJRice (edited 11-17-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||lennyb4||11-17-1999||12:02 PM||||Not to sound like a moron or anything like that, BUT once again i went out and looked all over the place and there is no bleeder on my slave cylinder.I've bleed clutch systems before and i've never seen one w/o a bleeder valve.This thing actually doesnt even look like it was made for my car, the cylinder mount looks smaller than the actual mount hole (piston hole in firewall). The bolt holes match (and works) but it just doesnt look right.The only things i can think of is to either bleed it at the line or replace it.I know you guys (and ladies) know alot more about this car than i do, if you have any other ideas please let me know.||||reg||11
Z||000008||Carrolles||11-17-1999||12:37 PM||||I'm confused.  What piston hole in the firewall are you referring to?  The Master cylinder mounts to a "firewall" in front of the driver, the slave cylinder mounts to the transmission housing.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Carrolles||11-17-1999||12:37 PM||||I'm confused.  What piston hole in the firewall are you referring to?  The Master cylinder mounts to a "firewall" in fron of the driver, the slave cylinder mounts to the transmission housing.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000010||lennyb4||11-17-1999||01:01 PM||||OK, Here's a brief discription of what i am talking about. When you open my trunk, directly inFront of the driver is the brakemaster cylinder & power brake booster. About 4-5" to the left (if your in the car)mounted to the firewall is a clutch cylinder, now i am assuming that this is my slave cylinder.And it does not have a bleeder valve anywhere on it.||||reg||5
Z||000011||Rodrv6||11-17-1999||01:05 PM||||Lenny, you are looking at the Master cylinder if you are in the front of the car.<BR>The slave cylinder is located on the transaxle in the back and should have a bleed fitting on the top of it.  I have had good luck bleeding the clutch on my '84 by using a large syringe to suction bleed at the bleed valve.||||reg||1
Z||000012||lennyb4||11-17-1999||01:22 PM||||I take back the part about not wanting to sound like a moron (duh, which way did he go?).I'll take a look around the trans-axle and see what i can find. Being new to this forum, i would like to say that this is the best place ive found yet. I mean you all are friendly,helpfull, informative,and a little wacky (from what ive read in the other topics). I am so glad i found this place.||||reg||1
